-- title:
--   Theorem 1 — global convergence of ALAVI (corrected: (iii) needs a cluster point in $\mathbb S_{MVIS}$)
-- statement:
--   Let $E,F$ be finite-dimensional real inner product spaces, $U\subseteq E$ a closed convex set, $C\subseteq F$ a closed convex cone with dual cone $C^*=\{p:\langle x,p\rangle\ge0\ \forall x\in C\}$, and let $G:E\to E$, $J:E\to(-\infty,+\infty]$, $\Theta:E\to F$ satisfy the standing assumptions: $G$ is $L$-Lipschitz on $U$, $J$ is proper convex l.s.c., $\Theta$ is $C$-convex and $\tau$-Lipschitz on $U$ ($\tau>0$). Let the parameters satisfy $\frac{\sqrt5-1}{2}\le\eta<1$, $0<\gamma<1/\tau$, $0<\alpha\le\frac{1}{2(\gamma\tau^2+L+\tau)\eta}$, and let $\{(v^k,u^k,p^k,q^k)\}$ be generated by ALAVI (25)–(28) from arbitrary $u^1,v^0,p^1$.
--
--   Suppose in addition Assumption B: the system (VIS) satisfies the primal-dual variational coherence condition, i.e. (MVIS) has a solution. Then:
--
--   1. the sequence $\{(v^k,u^k,p^k)\mid k=1,2,\dots\}$ is bounded;
--   2. every cluster point $(\bar v,\bar u,\bar p)$ of the sequence satisfies $\bar v=\bar u$, and $(\bar u,\bar p)$ solves (VIS);
--   3. if a cluster point $(\bar u,\bar u,\bar p)$ has $(\bar u,\bar p)\in\mathbb S_{MVIS}$, the entire sequence converges to $(\bar u,\bar u,\bar p)$;
--   4. if $\mathbb S_{VIS}\subseteq\mathbb S_{MVIS}$, the entire sequence converges to $(\bar u,\bar u,\bar p)$ for some $(\bar u,\bar p)\in\mathbb S_{VIS}$.
--
--   The paper's clause (iii) asserts convergence of the entire sequence to a solution of (VIS) under Assumption B alone. That is false: the proof applies (51) at a cluster point known only to solve (VIS), while (51) needs the Minty inequality there. In the counterexample of the mission description every assumption of the paper holds, every point of the unit circle is a cluster point, and the sequence does not converge. Clauses 3 and 4 are the minimal corrections; clause 4 is the form the printed proof tacitly assumes, and it holds when $G$ is monotone (p.7).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Cluster points are taken in the product topology of $E\times E\times F$, the topology of the stacked Euclidean norm.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: ℝⁿ and ℝᵐ with the Euclidean inner product → finite-dimensional real inner product spaces E, F; reason: ℝⁿ, ℝᵐ are instances and no coordinate is used, so the statement specializes to the page's
--   - generalization: G continuous on ℝⁿ and L-Lipschitz on U (H2); Θ continuous on ℝⁿ and τ-Lipschitz on an open O ⊇ U (H5) → G L-Lipschitz on U and Θ τ-Lipschitz on U, nothing assumed off U; reason: the proofs evaluate G and Θ only at points of U; the page's hypotheses imply these
--   - reading: J "a continuous function on U" (p.2) and "proper convex l.s.c." (H3, p.4) → J : E → (−∞, +∞] proper, convex, lower semicontinuous (IsProperConvexLSC); reason: the two printed descriptions differ; (H3) is the one the proofs use, and a convex J continuous on the closed set U, extended by +∞, satisfies it
--   - encoding: real values J(u) → (J u).toReal at points of dom J (effDom J); reason: J is finite wherever the statement evaluates it; at points outside dom J the printed inequalities read +∞ ≥ 0
--   - encoding: C a nonempty closed convex cone; C∗ (never defined on the page); Π the projection onto C∗ → C : ProperCone ℝ F; C∗ = ProperCone.innerDual C; Π = CalamaiMore.Convergence.proj onto C∗; reason: every nonempty closed convex cone is a ProperCone; C∗ is used as the dual cone {p | ⟨x, p⟩ ≥ 0 ∀ x ∈ C} (p.7, Example A.4); the projection onto the nonempty closed convex C∗ exists
--   - generalization: ALAVI input u¹, v⁰ ∈ ℝⁿ, p⁰ ∈ ℝᵐ, with p¹ read in (26) at k = 1 but never defined → u¹, v⁰ and p¹ arbitrary; reason: covers both readings p¹ = Π(p⁰ + γΘ(u¹)) and p¹ = p⁰
--   - generalization: Assumption A, including (H1) S_VIP ≠ ∅ and (H6) the constraint qualification → (H1) and (H6) not assumed; reason: (H6) is not used in the proof; (H1) follows from Assumption B by Proposition 4
--   - encoding: Assumption B: (VIS) satisfies the primal-dual variational coherence condition (Definition 2) → ∃ a b, IsMVIS U C G J Θ a b; reason: Definition 2 is exactly S_MVIS ≠ ∅
--   - generalization: u∗ a solution of (VIP) and (u∗, p∗) a saddle point of L_{u∗} over U × C∗ (hypotheses of Theorem 1) → not assumed; reason: they play no role once (51) is taken at a point of S_MVIS, which Assumption B supplies; the statement without them specializes to the page's
--   - correction: (iii) The entire sequence {(vᵏ, uᵏ, pᵏ) | k = 1, 2, ...} converges to a solution of (VIS) → (iii) if a cluster point (ū, ū, p̄) has (ū, p̄) ∈ S_MVIS, the sequence converges to it; (iii') if S_VIS ⊆ S_MVIS, the sequence converges to (ū, ū, p̄) with (ū, p̄) ∈ S_VIS; reason: the printed (iii) is false: with ℝ² ≅ ℂ, m = 1, C = ℝ₊, U = ℝ², J = 0, Θ(u) = |u| − 3, G(u) = g(|u|)·iu, g(r) = min(max(r − 1, −1), 1) (3-Lipschitz), η = 0.7, γ = 1/2, α = 0.1, every assumption holds ((0, 0) ∈ S_MVIS), and a run with p¹ = 0, |u¹| slightly above 1 and a suitable v⁰ has |uᵏ| → 1 with arg uᵏ → −∞ in steps tending to 0, so it has every point of the unit circle as a cluster point and does not converge
--   - generalization: (ii) every cluster point is a solution of (VIS) → every cluster point (v̄, ū, p̄) has v̄ = ū and (ū, p̄) solves (VIS); reason: the proof shows v̄ = ū; the added equality only strengthens the clause
-- source:
--   Zhao, Zhu & Zhang, An Augmented Lagrangian Approach to Conically Constrained Non-monotone Variational Inequality Problems, arXiv:2306.01214v1, Theorem 1, pp.17–18 (proof p.18)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_GoldenRatioVI_Fixed_solutionSet
import Definitions.Def_CalamaiMore_Convergence_proj
import Definitions.Def_ALAVI_Standing
import Definitions.Def_ALAVI_Params
import Definitions.Def_ALAVI_IsALAVIRun
import Definitions.Def_ALAVI_IsVIS
import Definitions.Def_ALAVI_IsMVIS

namespace ALAVI

/-- **Theorem 1 (Global convergence of ALAVI method)** (corrected: (iii) needs a cluster point in
`S_MVIS`). Under Assumption A, the parameter window of Lemma 4.1 and Assumption B
(`S_MVIS ≠ ∅`), for a run `{(vᵏ, uᵏ, pᵏ)}` of ALAVI:
(i) the sequence `{(vᵏ, uᵏ, pᵏ) | k = 1, 2, …}` is bounded;
(ii) every cluster point `(v̄, ū, p̄)` has `v̄ = ū` and `(ū, p̄)` solves (VIS);
(iii) if a cluster point `(ū, ū, p̄)` has `(ū, p̄) ∈ S_MVIS`, the entire sequence converges to it;
(iii') if `S_VIS ⊆ S_MVIS`, the entire sequence converges to `(ū, ū, p̄)` for some `(ū, p̄) ∈ S_VIS`.

Zhao, Zhu & Zhang, arXiv:2306.01214v1, Theorem 1, pp.17–18 (proof p.18).

Formalization Notes: the printed (iii), "the entire sequence converges to a solution of (VIS)", is
false under Assumption B alone: its proof applies (51) at a cluster point known only to lie in
`S_VIS`, and (51) needs the Minty inequality (20) there. A counterexample with every assumption
of the paper is in the mission description. (iii) and (iii') are the minimal corrections. The
hypotheses "u* a solution of (VIP)" and "(u*, p*) a saddle point of `L_{u*}`" are not used and
are dropped; (H1) and (H6) are not assumed. Cluster points are of the triple, in the product
topology of `E × E × F` (the same topology as that of the stacked Euclidean norm). -/
theorem theorem_1 {E F : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
    [NormedAddCommGroup F] [InnerProductSpace ℝ F] [FiniteDimensional ℝ F]
    (U : Set E) (C : ProperCone ℝ F) (G : E → E) (J : E → EReal) (Θ : E → F) (L τ η α γ : ℝ) (v u : ℕ → E) (p q : ℕ → F)
    (hA : Standing U C G J Θ L τ) (hP : Params η α γ L τ)
    (hrun : IsALAVIRun U C G J Θ η α γ v u p q)
    (hB : ∃ a b, IsMVIS U C G J Θ a b) :
    Bornology.IsBounded ((fun k => (v k, u k, p k)) '' Set.Ici 1) ∧
      (∀ (vbar ubar : E) (pbar : F),
        MapClusterPt (vbar, ubar, pbar) Filter.atTop (fun k => (v k, u k, p k)) →
          vbar = ubar ∧ IsVIS U C G J Θ ubar pbar) ∧
      (∀ (ubar : E) (pbar : F),
        MapClusterPt (ubar, ubar, pbar) Filter.atTop (fun k => (v k, u k, p k)) →
          IsMVIS U C G J Θ ubar pbar →
            Filter.Tendsto (fun k => (v k, u k, p k)) Filter.atTop (nhds (ubar, ubar, pbar))) ∧
      ((∀ (a : E) (b : F), IsVIS U C G J Θ a b → IsMVIS U C G J Θ a b) →
        ∃ (ubar : E) (pbar : F), IsVIS U C G J Θ ubar pbar ∧
          Filter.Tendsto (fun k => (v k, u k, p k)) Filter.atTop (nhds (ubar, ubar, pbar))) := by sorry

end ALAVI
